line 1266`: `if isinstance(dtype, str):`\n    - `line 1277`: `return super().is_dtype(dtype)`\n- artifact: `pandas/core/indexes/base.py:Index.get_indexer_for`\n  - module: `pandas.core.indexes.base`\n  - definition: `lines 6041-6062`\n  - first observed in trace A: `line 6059`\n  - observed lines: `6059, 6060`\n  - trace activity: 12 events, 3 calls, 6 line events, 3 returns, 0 exceptions, max depth 8\n  - sample executed lines:\n    - `line 6059`: `if self._index_as_unique:`\n    - `line 6060`: `return self.get_indexer(target)`\n- artifact: `pandas/core/indexes/base.py:Index.inferred_type`\n  - module: `pandas.core.indexes.base`\n  - definition: `lines 2731-2735`\n  - first observed in trace A: `line 2735`\n  - observed lines: `2735`\n  - trace activity: 12 events, 4 calls, 4 line events, 4 returns, 0 exceptions, max depth 10\n  - sample executed lines:\n    - `line 2735`: `return lib.infer_dtype(self._values, skipna=False)`\n- artifact: `pandas/core/missing.py:clean_fill_method`\n  - module: `pandas.core.missing`\n  - definition: `lines 107-126`\n  - first observed in trace A: `line 109`\n  - observed lines: `109, 110`\n  - trace activity: 12 events, 3 calls, 6 line events, 3 returns, 0 exceptions, max depth 11\n  - sample executed lines:\n    - `line 109`: `if method in [None, \"asfreq\"]:`\n    - `line 110`: `return None`\n- Additional artifact candidates omitted: `21`"}, {"content": "The issue is about adding the ability to group multiple columns on the same subplot when using `df.plot(subplots=True)` in pandas. This involves modifying the plotting functionality in pandas to accept a list of tuples indicating which columns should be grouped together.\n\nGiven the supplemental context, the artifact `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:<genexpr>` seems particularly relevant as it mentions `self.subplots`, which could be where the subplot logic resides. Let's investigate this file and its surrounding lines to understand the plotting mechanism.\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=file_path_or_pattern>pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py</parameter>\n<parameter=line_nums>[900, 901, 902, 903, 904]</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:line 900, 901, 902, 903, 904\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:MPLPlot._parse_errorbars`.\nSource: Retrieved code context including pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:line 900, 901, 902, 903, 904.\n```\n852 | def _parse_errorbars(self, label, err):\n853 |         \"\"\"\n854 |         Look for error keyword arguments and return the actual errorbar data\n855 |         or return the error DataFrame/dict\n856 | \n857 |         Error bars can be specified in several ways:\n858 |             Series: the user provides a pandas.Series object of the same\n859 |                     length as the data\n860 |             ndarray: provides a np.ndarray of the same length as the data\n861 |             DataFrame/dict: error values are paired with keys matching the\n862 |                     key in the plotted DataFrame\n863 |             str: the name of the column within the plotted DataFrame\n864 | \n865 |         Asymmetrical error bars are also supported, however raw error values\n866 |         must be provided in this case. For a ``N`` length :class:`Series`, a\n867 |         ``2xN`` array should be provided indicating lower and upper (or left\n868 |         and right) errors. For a ``MxN`` :class:`DataFrame`, asymmetrical errors\n869 |         should be in a ``Mx2xN`` array.\n870 |         \"\"\"\n871 |         if err is None:\n872 |             return None\n873 | \n874 |         def match_labels(data, e):\n875 |             e = e.reindex(data.index)\n876 |             return e\n877 | \n878 |         # key-matched DataFrame\n879 |         if isinstance(err, ABCDataFrame):\n880 | \n881 |             err = match_labels(self.data, err)\n882 |         # key-matched dict\n883 |         elif isinstance(err, dict):\n884 |             pass\n885 | \n886 |         # Series of error values\n887 |         elif isinstance(err, ABCSeries):\n888 |             # broadcast error series across data\n889 |             err = match_labels(self.data, err)\n890 |             err = np.atleast_2d(err)\n891 |             err = np.tile(err, (self.nseries, 1))\n892 | \n893 |         # errors are a column in the dataframe\n894 |         elif isinstance(err, str):\n895 |             evalues = self.data[err].values\n896 |             self.data = self.data[self.data.columns.drop(err)]\n897 |             err = np.atleast_2d(evalues)\n898 |             err = np.tile(err, (self.nseries, 1))\n899 | \n900 |         elif is_list_like(err):\n901 |             if is_iterator(err):\n902 |                 err = np.atleast_2d(list(err))\n903 |             else:\n904 |                 # raw error values\n905 |                 err = np.atleast_2d(err)\n906 | \n907 |             err_shape = err.shape\n908 | \n909 |             # asymmetrical error bars\n910 |             if isinstance(self.data, ABCSeries) and err_shape[0] == 2:\n911 |                 err = np.expand_dims(err, 0)\n912 |                 err_shape = err.shape\n913 |                 if err_shape[2] != len(self.data):\n914 |                     raise ValueError(\n915 |                         \"Asymmetrical error bars should be provided \"\n916 |                         f\"with the shape (2, {len(self.data)})\"\n917 |                     )\n918 |             elif isinstance(self.data, ABCDataFrame) and err.ndim == 3:\n919 |                 if (\n920 |                     (err_shape[0] != self.nseries)\n921 |                     or (err_shape[1] != 2)\n922 |                     or (err_shape[2] != len(self.data))\n923 |                 ):\n924 |                     raise ValueError(\n925 |                         \"Asymmetrical error bars should be provided \"\n926 |                         f\"with the shape ({self.nseries}, 2, {len(self.data)})\"\n927 |                     )\n928 | \n929 |             # broadcast errors to each data series\n930 |             if len(err) == 1:\n931 |                 err = np.tile(err, (self.nseries, 1))\n932 | \n933 |         elif is_number(err):\n934 |             err = np.tile([err], (self.nseries, len(self.data)))\n935 | \n936 |         else:\n937 |             msg = f\"No valid {label} detected\"\n938 |             raise ValueError(msg)\n939 | \n940 |         return err\n```\n"}, {"content": "It seems that the snippet provided is not directly related to subplot grouping. Let's explore the `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py` file further to find where the subplot creation logic is implemented.\n\nSince we are looking for the `subplots` functionality, let's search for the term `subplots` within this file.\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=file_path_or_pattern>pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py</parameter>\n<parameter=search_terms>[\"subplots\"]</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\npandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:LinePlot._post_plot_logic\npandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:MPLPlot._get_subplots\npandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:MPLPlot._get_axes_layout\npandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:MPLPlot._make_legend\n##Searching for term \"subplots\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/tools.py:create_subplots`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n132 | def create_subplots(\n133 |     naxes: int,\n134 |     sharex: bool = False,\n135 |     sharey: bool = False,\n136 |     squeeze: bool = True,\n137 |     subplot_kw=None,\n138 |     ax=None,\n139 |     layout=None,\n140 |     layout_type: str = \"box\",\n141 |     **fig_kw,\n142 | ):\n143 |     \"\"\"\n144 |     Create a figure with a set of subplots already made.\n145 | \n146 |     This utility wrapper makes it convenient to create common layouts of\n147 |     subplots, including the enclosing figure object, in a single call.\n148 | \n149 |     Parameters\n150 |     ----------\n151 |     naxes : int\n152 |       Number of required axes. Exceeded axes are set invisible. Default is\n153 |       nrows * ncols.\n154 | \n155 |     sharex : bool\n156 |       If True, the X axis will be shared amongst all subplots.\n157 | \n158 |     sharey : bool\n159 |       If True, the Y axis will be shared amongst all subplots.\n160 | \n161 |     squeeze : bool\n162 | \n163 |       If True, extra dimensions are squeezed out from the returned axis object:\n164 |         - if only one subplot is constructed (nrows=ncols=1), the resulting\n165 |         single Axis object is returned as a scalar.\n166 |         - for Nx1 or 1xN subplots, the returned object is a 1-d numpy object\n167 |         array of Axis objects are returned as numpy 1-d arrays.\n168 |         - for NxM subplots with N>1 and M>1 are returned as a 2d array.\n169 | \n170 |       If False, no squeezing is done: the returned axis object is always\n171 |       a 2-d array containing Axis instances, even if it ends up being 1x1.\n172 | \n173 |     subplot_kw : dict\n174 |       Dict with keywords passed to the add_subplot() call used to create each\n175 |       subplots.\n176 | \n177 |     ax : Matplotlib axis object, optional\n178 | \n179 |     layout : tuple\n180 |       Number of rows and columns of the subplot grid.\n181 |       If not specified, calculated from naxes and layout_type\n182 | \n183 |     layout_type : {'box', 'horizontal', 'vertical'}, default 'box'\n184 |       Specify how to layout the subplot grid.\n185 | \n186 |     fig_kw : Other keyword arguments to be passed to the figure() call.\n187 |         Note that all keywords not recognized above will be\n188 |         automatically included here.\n189 | \n190 |     Returns\n191 |     -------\n192 |     fig, ax : tuple\n193 |       - fig is the Matplotlib Figure object\n194 |       - ax can be either a single axis object or an array of axis objects if\n195 |       more than one subplot was created.  The dimensions of the resulting array\n196 |       can be controlled with the squeeze keyword, see above.\n197 | \n198 |     Examples\n199 |     --------\n200 |     x = np.linspace(0, 2*np.pi, 400)\n201 |     y = np.sin(x**2)\n202 | \n203 |     # Just a figure and one subplot\n204 |     f, ax = plt.subplots()\n205 |     ax.plot(x, y)\n206 |     ax.set_title('Simple plot')\n207 | \n208 |     # Two subplots, unpack the output array immediately\n209 |     f, (ax1, ax2) = plt.subplots(1, 2, sharey=True)\n210 |     ax1.plot(x, y)\n211 |     ax1.set_title('Sharing Y axis')\n212 |     ax2.scatter(x, y)\n213 | \n214 |     # Four polar axes\n215 |     plt.subplots(2, 2, subplot_kw=dict(polar=True))\n216 |     \"\"\"\n217 |     import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n218 | \n219 |     if subplot_kw is None:\n220 |         subplot_kw = {}\n221 | \n222 |     if ax is None:\n223 |         fig = plt.figure(**fig_kw)\n224 |     else:\n225 |         if is_list_like(ax):\n226 |             if squeeze:\n227 |                 ax = flatten_axes(ax)\n228 |             if layout is not None:\n229 |                 warnings.warn(\n230 |                     \"When passing multiple axes, layout keyword is ignored.\",\n231 |                     UserWarning,\n232 |                 )\n233 |             if sharex or sharey:\n234 |                 warnings.warn(\n235 |                     \"When passing multiple axes, sharex and sharey \"\n236 |                     \"are ignored. These settings must be specified when creating axes.\",\n237 |                     UserWarning,\n238 |                     stacklevel=find_stack_level(),\n239 |                 )\n240 |             if ax.size == naxes:\n241 |                 fig = ax.flat[0].get_figure()\n242 |                 return fig, ax\n243 |             else:\n244 |                 raise ValueError(\n245 |                     f\"The number of passed axes must be {naxes}, the \"\n246 |                     \"same as the output plot\"\n247 |                 )\n248 | \n249 |         fig = ax.get_figure()\n250 |         # if ax is passed and a number of subplots is 1, return ax as it is\n251 |         if naxes == 1:\n252 |             if squeeze:\n253 |                 return fig, ax\n254 |             else:\n255 |                 return fig, flatten_axes(ax)\n256 |         else:\n257 |             warnings.warn(\n258 |                 \"To output multiple subplots, the figure containing \"\n259 |                 \"the passed axes is being cleared.\",\n260 |                 UserWarning,\n261 |                 stacklevel=find_stack_level(),\n262 |             )\n263 |             fig.clear()\n264 | \n265 |     nrows, ncols = _get_layout(naxes, layout=layout, layout_type=layout_type)\n266 |     nplots = nrows * ncols\n267 | \n268 |     # Create empty object array to hold all axes.  It's easiest to make it 1-d\n269 |     # so we can just append subplots upon creation, and then\n270 |     axarr = np.empty(nplots, dtype=object)\n271 | \n272 |     # Create first subplot separately, so we can share it if requested\n273 |     ax0 = fig.add_subplot(nrows, ncols, 1, **subplot_kw)\n274 | \n275 |     if sharex:\n276 |         subplot_kw[\"sharex\"] = ax0\n277 |     if sharey:\n278 |         subplot_kw[\"sharey\"] = ax0\n279 |     axarr[0] = ax0\n280 | \n281 |     # Note off-by-one counting because add_subplot uses the MATLAB 1-based\n282 |     # convention.\n283 |     for i in range(1, nplots):\n284 |         kwds = subplot_kw.copy()\n285 |         # Set sharex and sharey to None for blank/dummy axes, these can\n286 |         # interfere with proper axis limits on the visible axes if\n287 |         # they share axes e.g. issue #7528\n288 |         if i >= naxes:\n289 |             kwds[\"sharex\"] = None\n290 |             kwds[\"sharey\"] = None\n291 |         ax = fig.add_subplot(nrows, ncols, i + 1, **kwds)\n292 |         axarr[i] = ax\n293 | \n294 |     if naxes != nplots:\n295 |         for ax in axarr[naxes:]:\n296 |             ax.set_visible(False)\n297 | \n298 |     handle_shared_axes(axarr, nplots, naxes, nrows, ncols, sharex, sharey)\n299 | \n300 |     if squeeze:\n301 |         # Reshape the array to have the final desired dimension (nrow,ncol),\n302 |         # though discarding unneeded dimensions that equal 1.  If we only have\n303 |         # one subplot, just return it instead of a 1-element array.\n304 |         if nplots == 1:\n305 |             axes = axarr[0]\n306 |         else:\n307 |             axes = axarr.reshape(nrows, ncols).squeeze()\n308 |     else:\n309 |         # returned axis array will be always 2-d, even if nrows=ncols=1\n310 |         axes = axarr.reshape(nrows, ncols)\n311 | \n312 |     return fig, axes\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:MPLPlot._setup_subplots`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n372 | def _setup_subplots(self):\n373 |         if self.subplots:\n374 |             fig, axes = create_subplots(\n375 |                 naxes=self.nseries,\n376 |                 sharex=self.sharex,\n377 |                 sharey=self.sharey,\n378 |                 figsize=self.figsize,\n379 |                 ax=self.ax,\n380 |                 layout=self.layout,\n381 |                 layout_type=self._layout_type,\n382 |             )\n383 |         else:\n384 |             if self.ax is None:\n385 |                 fig = self.plt.figure(figsize=self.figsize)\n386 |                 axes = fig.add_subplot(111)\n387 |             else:\n388 |                 fig = self.ax.get_figure()\n389 |                 if self.figsize is not None:\n390 |                     fig.set_size_inches(self.figsize)\n391 |                 axes = self.ax\n392 | \n393 |         axes = flatten_axes(axes)\n394 | \n395 |         valid_log = {False, True, \"sym\", None}\n396 |         input_log = {self.logx, self.logy, self.loglog}\n397 |         if input_log - valid_log:\n398 |             invalid_log = next(iter(input_log - valid_log))\n399 |             raise ValueError(\n400 |                 f\"Boolean, None and 'sym' are valid options, '{invalid_log}' is given.\"\n401 |             )\n402 | \n403 |         if self.logx is True or self.loglog is True:\n404 |             [a.set_xscale(\"log\") for a in axes]\n405 |         elif self.logx == \"sym\" or self.loglog == \"sym\":\n406 |             [a.set_xscale(\"symlog\") for a in axes]\n407 | \n408 |         if self.logy is True or self.loglog is True:\n409 |             [a.set_yscale(\"log\") for a in axes]\n410 |         elif self.logy == \"sym\" or self.loglog == \"sym\":\n411 |             [a.set_yscale(\"symlog\") for a in axes]\n412 | \n413 |         self.fig = fig\n414 |         self.axes = axes\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:MPLPlot._adorn_subplots`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n544 | def _adorn_subplots(self):\n545 |         \"\"\"Common post process unrelated to data\"\"\"\n546 |         if len(self.axes) > 0:\n547 |             all_axes = self._get_subplots()\n548 |             nrows, ncols = self._get_axes_layout()\n549 |             handle_shared_axes(\n550 |                 axarr=all_axes,\n551 |                 nplots=len(all_axes),\n552 |                 naxes=nrows * ncols,\n553 |                 nrows=nrows,\n554 |                 ncols=ncols,\n555 |                 sharex=self.sharex,\n556 |                 sharey=self.sharey,\n557 |             )\n558 | \n559 |         for ax in self.axes:\n560 |             if self.yticks is not None:\n561 |                 ax.set_yticks(self.yticks)\n562 | \n563 |             if self.xticks is not None:\n564 |                 ax.set_xticks(self.xticks)\n565 | \n566 |             if self.ylim is not None:\n567 |                 ax.set_ylim(self.ylim)\n568 | \n569 |             if self.xlim is not None:\n570 |                 ax.set_xlim(self.xlim)\n571 | \n572 |             # GH9093, currently Pandas does not show ylabel, so if users provide\n573 |             # ylabel will set it as ylabel in the plot.\n574 |             if self.ylabel is not None:\n575 |                 ax.set_ylabel(pprint_thing(self.ylabel))\n576 | \n577 |             ax.grid(self.grid)\n578 | \n579 |         if self.title:\n580 |             if self.subplots:\n581 |                 if is_list_like(self.title):\n582 |                     if len(self.title) != self.nseries:\n583 |                         raise ValueError(\n584 |                             \"The length of `title` must equal the number \"\n585 |                             \"of columns if using `title` of type `list` \"\n586 |                             \"and `subplots=True`.\\n\"\n587 |                             f\"length of title = {len(self.title)}\\n\"\n588 |                             f\"number of columns = {self.nseries}\"\n589 |                         )\n590 | \n591 |                     for (ax, title) in zip(self.axes, self.title):\n592 |                         ax.set_title(title)\n593 |                 else:\n594 |                     self.fig.suptitle(self.title)\n595 |             else:\n596 |                 if is_list_like(self.title):\n597 |                     msg = (\n598 |                         \"Using `title` of type `list` is not supported \"\n599 |                         \"unless `subplots=True` is passed\"\n600 |                     )\n601 |                     raise ValueError(msg)\n602 |                 self.axes[0].set_title(self.title)\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:MPLPlot._make_legend`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n650 | def _make_legend(self) -> None:\n651 |         ax, leg = self._get_ax_legend(self.axes[0])\n652 | \n653 |         handles = []\n654 |         labels = []\n655 |         title = \"\"\n656 | \n657 |         if not self.subplots:\n658 |             if leg is not None:\n659 |                 title = leg.get_title().get_text()\n660 |                 # Replace leg.LegendHandles because it misses marker info\n661 |                 handles = leg.legendHandles\n662 |                 labels = [x.get_text() for x in leg.get_texts()]\n663 | \n664 |             if self.legend:\n665 |                 if self.legend == \"reverse\":\n666 |                     handles += reversed(self.legend_handles)\n667 |                     labels += reversed(self.legend_labels)\n668 |                 else:\n669 |                     handles += self.legend_handles\n670 |                     labels += self.legend_labels\n671 | \n672 |                 if self.legend_title is not None:\n673 |                     title = self.legend_title\n674 | \n675 |             if len(handles) > 0:\n676 |                 ax.legend(handles, labels, loc=\"best\", title=title)\n677 | \n678 |         elif self.subplots and self.legend:\n679 |             for ax in self.axes:\n680 |                 if ax.get_visible():\n681 |                     ax.legend(loc=\"best\")\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:MPLPlot._get_subplots`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n961 | def _get_subplots(self):\n962 |         from matplotlib.axes import Subplot\n963 | \n964 |         return [\n965 |             ax for ax in self.axes[0].get_figure().get_axes() if isinstance(ax, Subplot)\n966 |         ]\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:MPLPlot._get_axes_layout`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n968 | def _get_axes_layout(self) -> tuple[int, int]:\n969 |         axes = self._get_subplots()\n970 |         x_set = set()\n971 |         y_set = set()\n972 |         for ax in axes:\n973 |             # check axes coordinates to estimate layout\n974 |             points = ax.get_position().get_points()\n975 |             x_set.add(points[0][0])\n976 |             y_set.add(points[0][1])\n977 |         return (len(y_set), len(x_set))\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:LinePlot._post_plot_logic`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1308 | def _post_plot_logic(self, ax: Axes, data):\n1309 |         from matplotlib.ticker import FixedLocator\n1310 | \n1311 |         def get_label(i):\n1312 |             if is_float(i) and i.is_integer():\n1313 |                 i = int(i)\n1314 |             try:\n1315 |                 return pprint_thing(data.index[i])\n1316 |             except Exception:\n1317 |                 return \"\"\n1318 | \n1319 |         if self._need_to_set_index:\n1320 |             xticks = ax.get_xticks()\n1321 |             xticklabels = [get_label(x) for x in xticks]\n1322 |             ax.xaxis.set_major_locator(FixedLocator(xticks))\n1323 |             ax.set_xticklabels(xticklabels)\n1324 | \n1325 |         # If the index is an irregular time series, then by default\n1326 |         # we rotate the tick labels. The exception is if there are\n1327 |         # subplots which don't share their x-axes, in which we case\n1328 |         # we don't rotate the ticklabels as by default the subplots\n1329 |         # would be too close together.\n1330 |         condition = (\n1331 |             not self._use_dynamic_x()\n1332 |             and (data.index._is_all_dates and self.use_index)\n1333 |             and (not self.subplots or (self.subplots and self.sharex))\n1334 |         )\n1335 | \n1336 |         index_name = self._get_index_name()\n1337 | \n1338 |         if condition:\n1339 |             # irregular TS rotated 30 deg. by default\n1340 |             # probably a better place to check / set this.\n1341 |             if not self._rot_set:\n1342 |                 self.rot = 30\n1343 |             format_date_labels(ax, rot=self.rot)\n1344 | \n1345 |         if index_name is not None and self.use_index:\n1346 |             ax.set_xlabel(index_name)\n```\n"}, {"content": "The relevant functions for handling subplots in `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py` are `_setup_subplots`, `_adorn_subplots`, and `_make_legend`. Additionally, the `create_subplots` function in `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/tools.py` is involved in creating the subplots. Let's now examine these files and their surrounding areas for potential modifications to support grouping columns in subplots.\n\nFirst, let's look closer at `_setup_subplots` to understand how subplots are initialized and potentially modified to accept grouping.\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=file_path_or_pattern>pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py</parameter>\n<parameter=line_nums>[370, 371, 372, 373, 374, 375, 376, 377, 378, 379, 380, 381, 382, 383]</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:line 370, 371, 372, 373, 374, 375, 376, 377, 378, 379, 380, 381, 382, 383\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound class `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:MPLPlot`.\nSource: Retrieved code context including pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/core.py:line 370, 371, 372, 373, 374, 375, 376, 377, 378, 379, 380, 381, 382, 383.\nJust show the structure of this class due to response length limitations:\n```\nclass MPLPlot:\n\n    @property\n    def _kind(self):\n...\n    _layout_type = \"vertical\"\n    _default_rot = 0\n    orientation: str | None = None\n\n    axes: np.ndarray  # of Axes objects\n\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        data,\n        kind=None,\n        by: IndexLabel | None = None,\n        subplots=False,\n        sharex=None,\n        sharey=False,\n        use_index=True,