998 |         Rewrite *self* using a defined rule.\n1999 | \n2000 |         Rewriting transforms an expression to another, which is mathematically\n2001 |         equivalent but structurally different. For example you can rewrite\n2002 |         trigonometric functions as complex exponentials or combinatorial\n2003 |         functions as gamma function.\n2004 | \n2005 |         This method takes a *pattern* and a *rule* as positional arguments.\n2006 |         *pattern* is optional parameter which defines the types of expressions\n2007 |         that will be transformed. If it is not passed, all possible expressions\n2008 |         will be rewritten. *rule* defines how the expression will be rewritten.\n2009 | \n2010 |         Parameters\n2011 |         ==========\n2012 | \n2013 |         args : Expr\n2014 |             A *rule*, or *pattern* and *rule*.\n2015 |             - *pattern* is a type or an iterable of types.\n2016 |             - *rule* can be any object.\n2017 | \n2018 |         deep : bool, optional\n2019 |             If ``True``, subexpressions are recursively transformed. Default is\n2020 |             ``True``.\n2021 | \n2022 |         Examples\n2023 |         ========\n2024 | \n2025 |         If *pattern* is unspecified, all possible expressions are transformed.\n2026 | \n2027 |         >>> from sympy import cos, sin, exp, I\n2028 |         >>> from sympy.abc import x\n2029 |         >>> expr = cos(x) + I*sin(x)\n2030 |         >>> expr.rewrite(exp)\n2031 |         exp(I*x)\n2032 | \n2033 |         Pattern can be a type or an iterable of types.\n2034 | \n2035 |         >>> expr.rewrite(sin, exp)\n2036 |         exp(I*x)/2 + cos(x) - exp(-I*x)/2\n2037 |         >>> expr.rewrite([cos,], exp)\n2038 |         exp(I*x)/2 + I*sin(x) + exp(-I*x)/2\n2039 |         >>> expr.rewrite([cos, sin], exp)\n2040 |         exp(I*x)\n2041 | \n2042 |         Rewriting behavior can be implemented by defining ``_eval_rewrite()``\n2043 |         method.\n2044 | \n2045 |         >>> from sympy import Expr, sqrt, pi\n2046 |         >>> class MySin(Expr):\n2047 |         ...     def _eval_rewrite(self, rule, args, **hints):\n2048 |         ...         x, = args\n2049 |         ...         if rule == cos:\n2050 |         ...             return cos(pi/2 - x, evaluate=False)\n2051 |         ...         if rule == sqrt:\n2052 |         ...             return sqrt(1 - cos(x)**2)\n2053 |         >>> MySin(MySin(x)).rewrite(cos)\n2054 |         cos(-cos(-x + pi/2) + pi/2)\n2055 |         >>> MySin(x).rewrite(sqrt)\n2056 |         sqrt(1 - cos(x)**2)\n2057 | \n2058 |         Defining ``_eval_rewrite_as_[...]()`` method is supported for backwards\n2059 |         compatibility reason. This may be removed in the future and using it is\n2060 |         discouraged.\n2061 | \n2062 |         >>> class MySin(Expr):\n2063 |         ...     def _eval_rewrite_as_cos(self, *args, **hints):\n2064 |         ...         x, = args\n2065 |         ...         return cos(pi/2 - x, evaluate=False)\n2066 |         >>> MySin(x).rewrite(cos)\n2067 |         cos(-x + pi/2)\n2068 | \n2069 |         \"\"\"\n2070 |         if not args:\n2071 |             return self\n2072 | \n2073 |         hints.update(deep=deep)\n2074 | \n2075 |         pattern = args[:-1]\n2076 |         rule = args[-1]\n2077 | \n2078 |         # support old design by _eval_rewrite_as_[...] method\n2079 |         if isinstance(rule, str):\n2080 |             method = \"_eval_rewrite_as_%s\" % rule\n2081 |         elif hasattr(rule, \"__name__\"):\n2082 |             # rule is class or function\n2083 |             clsname = rule.__name__\n2084 |             method = \"_eval_rewrite_as_%s\" % clsname\n2085 |         else:\n2086 |             # rule is instance\n2087 |             clsname = rule.__class__.__name__\n2088 |             method = \"_eval_rewrite_as_%s\" % clsname\n2089 | \n2090 |         if pattern:\n2091 |             if iterable(pattern[0]):\n2092 |                 pattern = pattern[0]\n2093 |             pattern = tuple(p for p in pattern if self.has(p))\n2094 |             if not pattern:\n2095 |                 return self\n2096 |         # hereafter, empty pattern is interpreted as all pattern.\n2097 | \n2098 |         return self._rewrite(pattern, rule, method, **hints)\n```\n\nFound function `sympy/core/expr.py:Expr.series`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n2868 | def series(self, x=None, x0=0, n=6, dir=\"+\", logx=None, cdir=0):\n2869 |         \"\"\"\n2870 |         Series expansion of \"self\" around ``x = x0`` yielding either terms of\n2871 |         the series one by one (the lazy series given when n=None), else\n2872 |         all the terms at once when n != None.\n2873 | \n2874 |         Returns the series expansion of \"self\" around the point ``x = x0``\n2875 |         with respect to ``x`` up to ``O((x - x0)**n, x, x0)`` (default n is 6).\n2876 | \n2877 |         If ``x=None`` and ``self`` is univariate, the univariate symbol will\n2878 |         be supplied, otherwise an error will be raised.\n2879 | \n2880 |         Parameters\n2881 |         ==========\n2882 | \n2883 |         expr : Expression\n2884 |                The expression whose series is to be expanded.\n2885 | \n2886 |         x : Symbol\n2887 |             It is the variable of the expression to be calculated.\n2888 | \n2889 |         x0 : Value\n2890 |              The value around which ``x`` is calculated. Can be any value\n2891 |              from ``-oo`` to ``oo``.\n2892 | \n2893 |         n : Value\n2894 |             The value used to represent the order in terms of ``x**n``,\n2895 |             up to which the series is to be expanded.\n2896 | \n2897 |         dir : String, optional\n2898 |               The series-expansion can be bi-directional. If ``dir=\"+\"``,\n2899 |               then (x->x0+). If ``dir=\"-\", then (x->x0-). For infinite\n2900 |               ``x0`` (``oo`` or ``-oo``), the ``dir`` argument is determined\n2901 |               from the direction of the infinity (i.e., ``dir=\"-\"`` for\n2902 |               ``oo``).\n2903 | \n2904 |         logx : optional\n2905 |                It is used to replace any log(x) in the returned series with a\n2906 |                symbolic value rather than evaluating the actual value.\n2907 | \n2908 |         cdir : optional\n2909 |                It stands for complex direction, and indicates the direction\n2910 |                from which the expansion needs to be evaluated.\n2911 | \n2912 |         Examples\n2913 |         ========\n2914 | \n2915 |         >>> from sympy import cos, exp, tan\n2916 |         >>> from sympy.abc import x, y\n2917 |         >>> cos(x).series()\n2918 |         1 - x**2/2 + x**4/24 + O(x**6)\n2919 |         >>> cos(x).series(n=4)\n2920 |         1 - x**2/2 + O(x**4)\n2921 |         >>> cos(x).series(x, x0=1, n=2)\n2922 |         cos(1) - (x - 1)*sin(1) + O((x - 1)**2, (x, 1))\n2923 |         >>> e = cos(x + exp(y))\n2924 |         >>> e.series(y, n=2)\n2925 |         cos(x + 1) - y*sin(x + 1) + O(y**2)\n2926 |         >>> e.series(x, n=2)\n2927 |         cos(exp(y)) - x*sin(exp(y)) + O(x**2)\n2928 | \n2929 |         If ``n=None`` then a generator of the series terms will be returned.\n2930 | \n2931 |         >>> term=cos(x).series(n=None)\n2932 |         >>> [next(term) for i in range(2)]\n2933 |         [1, -x**2/2]\n2934 | \n2935 |         For ``dir=+`` (default) the series is calculated from the right and\n2936 |         for ``dir=-`` the series from the left. For smooth functions this\n2937 |         flag will not alter the results.\n2938 | \n2939 |         >>> abs(x).series(dir=\"+\")\n2940 |         x\n2941 |         >>> abs(x).series(dir=\"-\")\n2942 |         -x\n2943 |         >>> f = tan(x)\n2944 |         >>> f.series(x, 2, 6, \"+\")\n2945 |         tan(2) + (1 + tan(2)**2)*(x - 2) + (x - 2)**2*(tan(2)**3 + tan(2)) +\n2946 |         (x - 2)**3*(1/3 + 4*tan(2)**2/3 + tan(2)**4) + (x - 2)**4*(tan(2)**5 +\n2947 |         5*tan(2)**3/3 + 2*tan(2)/3) + (x - 2)**5*(2/15 + 17*tan(2)**2/15 +\n2948 |         2*tan(2)**4 + tan(2)**6) + O((x - 2)**6, (x, 2))\n2949 | \n2950 |         >>> f.series(x, 2, 3, \"-\")\n2951 |         tan(2) + (2 - x)*(-tan(2)**2 - 1) + (2 - x)**2*(tan(2)**3 + tan(2))\n2952 |         + O((x - 2)**3, (x, 2))\n2953 | \n2954 |         For rational expressions this method may return original expression without the Order term.\n2955 |         >>> (1/x).series(x, n=8)\n2956 |         1/x\n2957 | \n2958 |         Returns\n2959 |         =======\n2960 | \n2961 |         Expr : Expression\n2962 |             Series expansion of the expression about x0\n2963 | \n2964 |         Raises\n2965 |         ======\n2966 | \n2967 |         TypeError\n2968 |             If \"n\" and \"x0\" are infinity objects\n2969 | \n2970 |         PoleError\n2971 |             If \"x0\" is an infinity object\n2972 | \n2973 |         \"\"\"\n2974 |         if x is None:\n2975 |             syms = self.free_symbols\n2976 |             if not syms:\n2977 |                 return self\n2978 |             elif len(syms) > 1:\n2979 |                 raise ValueError('x must be given for multivariate functions.')\n2980 |             x = syms.pop()\n2981 | \n2982 |         from .symbol import Dummy, Symbol\n2983 |         if isinstance(x, Symbol):\n2984 |             dep = x in self.free_symbols\n2985 |         else:\n2986 |             d = Dummy()\n2987 |             dep = d in self.xreplace({x: d}).free_symbols\n2988 |         if not dep:\n2989 |             if n is None:\n2990 |                 return (s for s in [self])\n2991 |             else:\n2992 |                 return self\n2993 | \n2994 |         if len(dir) != 1 or dir not in '+-':\n2995 |             raise ValueError(\"Dir must be '+' or '-'\")\n2996 | \n2997 |         if n is not None:\n2998 |             n = int(n)\n2999 |             if n < 0:\n3000 |                 raise ValueError(\"Number of terms should be nonnegative\")\n3001 | \n3002 |         x0 = sympify(x0)\n3003 |         cdir = sympify(cdir)\n3004 |         from sympy.functions.elementary.complexes import im, sign\n3005 | \n3006 |         if not cdir.is_zero:\n3007 |             if cdir.is_real:\n3008 |                 dir = '+' if cdir.is_positive else '-'\n3009 |             else:\n3010 |                 dir = '+' if im(cdir).is_positive else '-'\n3011 |         else:\n3012 |             if x0 and x0.is_infinite:\n3013 |                 cdir = sign(x0).simplify()\n3014 |             elif str(dir) == \"+\":\n3015 |                 cdir = S.One\n3016 |             elif str(dir) == \"-\":\n3017 |                 cdir = S.NegativeOne\n3018 |             elif cdir == S.Zero:\n3019 |                 cdir = S.One\n3020 | \n3021 |         cdir = cdir/abs(cdir)\n3022 | \n3023 |         if x0 and x0.is_infinite:\n3024 |             from .function import PoleError\n3025 |             try:\n3026 |                 s = self.subs(x, cdir/x).series(x, n=n, dir='+', cdir=1)\n3027 |                 if n is None:\n3028 |                     return (si.subs(x, cdir/x) for si in s)\n3029 |                 return s.subs(x, cdir/x)\n3030 |             except PoleError:\n3031 |                 s = self.subs(x, cdir*x).aseries(x, n=n)\n3032 |                 return s.subs(x, cdir*x)\n3033 | \n3034 |         # use rep to shift origin to x0 and change sign (if dir is negative)\n3035 |         # and undo the process with rep2\n3036 |         if x0 or cdir != 1:\n3037 |             s = self.subs({x: x0 + cdir*x}).series(x, x0=0, n=n, dir='+', logx=logx, cdir=1)\n3038 |             if n is None:  # lseries...\n3039 |                 return (si.subs({x: x/cdir - x0/cdir}) for si in s)\n3040 |             return s.subs({x: x/cdir - x0/cdir})\n3041 | \n3042 |         # from here on it's x0=0 and dir='+' handling\n3043 | \n3044 |         if x.is_positive is x.is_negative is None or x.is_Symbol is not True:\n3045 |             # replace x with an x that has a positive assumption\n3046 |             xpos = Dummy('x', positive=True)\n3047 |             rv = self.subs(x, xpos).series(xpos, x0, n, dir, logx=logx, cdir=cdir)\n3048 |             if n is None:\n3049 |                 return (s.subs(xpos, x) for s in rv)\n3050 |             else:\n3051 |                 return rv.subs(xpos, x)\n3052 | \n3053 |         from sympy.series.order import Order\n3054 |         if n is not None:  # nseries handling\n3055 |             s1 = self._eval_nseries(x, n=n, logx=logx, cdir=cdir)\n3056 |             o = s1.getO() or S.Zero\n3057 |             if o:\n3058 |                 # make sure the requested order is returned\n3059 |                 ngot = o.getn()\n3060 |                 if ngot > n:\n3061 |                     # leave o in its current form (e.g. with x*log(x)) so\n3062 |                     # it eats terms properly, then replace it below\n3063 |                     if n != 0:\n3064 |                         s1 += o.subs(x, x**Rational(n, ngot))\n3065 |                     else:\n3066 |                         s1 += Order(1, x)\n3067 |                 elif ngot < n:\n3068 |                     # increase the requested number of terms to get the desired\n3069 |                     # number keep increasing (up to 9) until the received order\n3070 |                     # is different than the original order and then predict how\n3071 |                     # many additional terms are needed\n3072 |                     from sympy.functions.elementary.integers import ceiling\n3073 |                     for more in range(1, 9):\n3074 |                         s1 = self._eval_nseries(x, n=n + more, logx=logx, cdir=cdir)\n3075 |                         newn = s1.getn()\n3076 |                         if newn != ngot:\n3077 |                             ndo = n + ceiling((n - ngot)*more/(newn - ngot))\n3078 |                             s1 = self._eval_nseries(x, n=ndo, logx=logx, cdir=cdir)\n3079 |                             while s1.getn() < n:\n3080 |                                 s1 = self._eval_nseries(x, n=ndo, logx=logx, cdir=cdir)\n3081 |                                 ndo += 1\n3082 |                             break\n3083 |                     else:\n3084 |                         raise ValueError('Could not calculate %s terms for %s'\n3085 |                                          % (str(n), self))\n3086 |                     s1 += Order(x**n, x)\n3087 |                 o = s1.getO()\n3088 |                 s1 = s1.removeO()\n3089 |             elif s1.has(Order):\n3090 |                 # asymptotic expansion\n3091 |                 return s1\n3092 |             else:\n3093 |                 o = Order(x**n, x)\n3094 |                 s1done = s1.doit()\n3095 |                 try:\n3096 |                     if (s1done + o).removeO() == s1done:\n3097 |                         o = S.Zero\n3098 |                 except NotImplementedError:\n3099 |                     return s1\n3100 | \n3101 |             try:\n3102 |                 from sympy.simplify.radsimp import collect\n3103 |                 return collect(s1, x) + o\n3104 |             except NotImplementedError:\n3105 |                 return s1 + o\n3106 | \n3107 |         else:  # lseries handling\n3108 |             def yield_lseries(s):\n3109 |                 \"\"\"Return terms of lseries one at a time.\"\"\"\n3110 |                 for si in s:\n3111 |                     if not si.is_Add:\n3112 |                         yield si\n3113 |                         continue\n3114 |                     # yield terms 1 at a time if possible\n3115 |                     # by increasing order until all the\n3116 |                     # terms have been returned\n3117 |                     yielded = 0\n3118 |                     o = Order(si, x)*x\n3119 |                     ndid = 0\n3120 |                     ndo = len(si.args)\n3121 |                     while 1:\n3122 |                         do = (si - yielded + o).removeO()\n3123 |                         o *= x\n3124 |                         if not do or do.is_Order:\n3125 |                             continue\n3126 |                         if do.is_Add:\n3127 |                             ndid += len(do.args)\n3128 |                         else:\n3129 |                             ndid += 1\n3130 |                         yield do\n3131 |                         if ndid == ndo:\n3132 |                             break\n3133 |                         yielded += do\n3134 | \n3135 |             return yield_lseries(self.removeO()._eval_lseries(x, logx=logx, cdir=cdir))\n```\n\nFound function `sympy/core/mul.py:Mul._eval_expand_mul`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n922 | def _eval_expand_mul(self, **hints):\n923 |         from sympy.simplify.radsimp import fraction\n924 | \n925 |         # Handle things like 1/(x*(x + 1)), which are automatically converted\n926 |         # to 1/x*1/(x + 1)\n927 |         expr = self\n928 |         # default matches fraction's default\n929 |         n, d = fraction(expr, hints.get('exact', False))\n930 |         if d.is_Mul:\n931 |             n, d = [i._eval_expand_mul(**hints) if i.is_Mul else i\n932 |                 for i in (n, d)]\n933 |         expr = n/d\n934 |         if not expr.is_Mul:\n935 |             return expr\n936 | \n937 |         plain, sums, rewrite = [], [], False\n938 |         for factor in expr.args:\n939 |             if factor.is_Add:\n940 |                 sums.append(factor)\n941 |                 rewrite = True\n942 |             else:\n943 |                 if factor.is_commutative:\n944 |                     plain.append(factor)\n945 |                 else:\n946 |                     sums.append(Basic(factor))  # Wrapper\n947 | \n948 |         if not rewrite:\n949 |             return expr\n950 |         else:\n951 |             plain = self.func(*plain)\n952 |             if sums:\n953 |                 deep = hints.get(\"deep\", False)\n954 |                 terms = self.func._expandsums(sums)\n955 |                 args = []\n956 |                 for term in terms:\n957 |                     t = self.func(plain, term)\n958 |                     if t.is_Mul and any(a.is_Add for a in t.args) and deep:\n959 |                         t = t._eval_expand_mul()\n960 |                     args.append(t)\n961 |                 return Add(*args)\n962 |             else:\n963 |                 return plain\n```\n"}, {"content": "To locate the specific files, classes, or functions relevant to modifying the `rewrite` method to handle `abs` by calling `_eval_rewrite_as_Abs`, we need to inspect the `Basic.rewrite` method in `sympy/core/basic.py` and the `Abs` class in `sympy/functions/elementary/complexes.py`. \n\nSince `Basic.rewrite` is mentioned and it's where the rewriting mechanism is handled, we should start by examining this method. Additionally, we should look at the `Abs` class to see how it's defined and how its rewriting method is implemented.\n\nLet's retrieve the full content of `sympy/core/basic.py:Basic.rewrite` and `sympy/functions/elementary/complexes.py:Abs` to get a better understanding.\n\n<function=get_entity_contents>\n<parameter=entity_names>\n['sympy/core/basic.py:Basic.rewrite', 'sympy/functions/elementary/complexes.py:Abs']\n</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "get_entity_contents", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for entity `sympy/core/basic.py:Basic.rewrite`...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sympy/core/basic.py:Basic.rewrite`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `sympy/core/basic.py:Basic.rewrite`.\n```\n1996 | def rewrite(self, *args, deep=True, **hints):\n1997 |         \"\"\"\n1998 |         Rewrite *self* using a defined rule.\n1999 | \n2000 |         Rewriting transforms an expression to another, which is mathematically\n2001 |         equivalent but structurally different. For example you can rewrite\n2002 |         trigonometric functions as complex exponentials or combinatorial\n2003 |         functions as gamma function.\n2004 | \n2005 |         This method takes a *pattern* and a *rule* as positional arguments.\n2006 |         *pattern* is optional parameter which defines the types of expressions\n2007 |         that will be transformed. If it is not passed, all possible expressions\n2008 |         will be rewritten. *rule* defines how the expression will be rewritten.\n2009 | \n2010 |         Parameters\n2011 |         ==========\n2012 | \n2013 |         args : Expr\n2014 |             A *rule*, or *pattern* and *rule*.\n2015 |             - *pattern* is a type or an iterable of types.\n2016 |             - *rule* can be any object.\n2017 | \n2018 |         deep : bool, optional\n2019 |             If ``True``, subexpressions are recursively transformed. Default is\n2020 |             ``True``.\n2021 | \n2022 |         Examples\n2023 |         ========\n2024 | \n2025 |         If *pattern* is unspecified, all possible expressions are transformed.\n2026 | \n2027 |         >>> from sympy import cos, sin, exp, I\n2028 |         >>> from sympy.abc import x\n2029 |         >>> expr = cos(x) + I*sin(x)\n2030 |         >>> expr.rewrite(exp)\n2031 |         exp(I*x)\n2032 | \n2033 |         Pattern can be a type or an iterable of types.\n2034 | \n2035 |         >>> expr.rewrite(sin, exp)\n2036 |         exp(I*x)/2 + cos(x) - exp(-I*x)/2\n2037 |         >>> expr.rewrite([cos,], exp)\n2038 |         exp(I*x)/2 + I*sin(x) + exp(-I*x)/2\n2039 |         >>> expr.rewrite([cos, sin], exp)\n2040 |         exp(I*x)\n2041 | \n2042 |         Rewriting behavior can be implemented by defining ``_eval_rewrite()``\n2043 |         method.\n2044 | \n2045 |         >>> from sympy import Expr, sqrt, pi\n2046 |         >>> class MySin(Expr):\n2047 |         ...     def _eval_rewrite(self, rule, args, **hints):\n2048 |         ...         x, = args\n2049 |         ...         if rule == cos:\n2050 |         ...             return cos(pi/2 - x, evaluate=False)\n2051 |         ...         if rule == sqrt:\n2052 |         ...             return sqrt(1 - cos(x)**2)\n2053 |         >>> MySin(MySin(x)).rewrite(cos)\n2054 |         cos(-cos(-x + pi/2) + pi/2)\n2055 |         >>> MySin(x).rewrite(sqrt)\n2056 |         sqrt(1 - cos(x)**2)\n2057 | \n2058 |         Defining ``_eval_rewrite_as_[...]()`` method is supported for backwards\n2059 |         compatibility reason. This may be removed in the future and using it is\n2060 |         discouraged.\n2061 | \n2062 |         >>> class MySin(Expr):\n2063 |         ...     def _eval_rewrite_as_cos(self, *args, **hints):\n2064 |         ...         x, = args\n2065 |         ...         return cos(pi/2 - x, evaluate=False)\n2066 |         >>> MySin(x).rewrite(cos)\n2067 |         cos(-x + pi/2)\n2068 | \n2069 |         \"\"\"\n2070 |         if not args:\n2071 |             return self\n2072 | \n2073 |         hints.update(deep=deep)\n2074 | \n2075 |         pattern = args[:-1]\n2076 |         rule = args[-1]\n2077 | \n2078 |         # support old design by _eval_rewrite_as_[...] method\n2079 |         if isinstance(rule, str):\n2080 |             method = \"_eval_rewrite_as_%s\" % rule\n2081 |         elif hasattr(rule, \"__name__\"):\n2082 |             # rule is class or function\n2083 |             clsname = rule.__name__\n2084 |             method = \"_eval_rewrite_as_%s\" % clsname\n2085 |         else:\n2086 |             # rule is instance\n2087 |             clsname = rule.__class__.__name__\n2088 |             method = \"_eval_rewrite_as_%s\" % clsname\n2089 | \n2090 |         if pattern:\n2091 |             if iterable(pattern[0]):\n2092 |                 pattern = pattern[0]\n2093 |             pattern = tuple(p for p in pattern if self.has(p))\n2094 |             if not pattern:\n2095 |                 return self\n2096 |         # hereafter, empty pattern is interpreted as all pattern.\n2097 | \n2098 |         return self._rewrite(pattern, rule, method, **hints)\n```\n\n\n##Searching for entity `sympy/functions/elementary/complexes.py:Abs`...\n### Search Result:\nFound class `sympy/functions/elementary/complexes.py:Abs`.\nSource: Exact match found for entity name `sympy/functions/elementary/complexes.py:Abs`.\n```\n446 | class Abs(DefinedFunction):\n447 |     \"\"\"\n448 |     Return the absolute value of the argument.\n449 | \n450 |     Explanation\n451 |     ===========\n452 | \n453 |     This is an extension of the built-in function ``abs()`` to accept symbolic\n454 |     values.  If you pass a SymPy expression to the built-in ``abs()``, it will\n455 |     pass it automatically to ``Abs()``.\n456 | \n457 |     Examples\n458 |     ========\n459 | \n460 |     >>> from sympy import Abs, Symbol, S, I\n461 |     >>> Abs(-1)\n462 |     1\n463 |     >>> x = Symbol('x', real=True)\n464 |     >>> Abs(-x)\n465 |     Abs(x)\n466 |     >>> Abs(x**2)\n467 |     x**2\n468 |     >>> abs(-x) # The Python built-in\n469 |     Abs(x)\n470 |     >>> Abs(3*x + 2*I)\n471 |     sqrt(9*x**2 + 4)\n472 |     >>> Abs(8*I)\n473 |     8\n474 | \n475 |     Note that the Python built-in will return either an Expr or int depending on\n476 |     the argument::\n477 | \n478 |         >>> type(abs(-1))\n479 |         <... 'int'>\n480 |         >>> type(abs(S.NegativeOne))\n481 |         <class 'sympy.core.numbers.One'>\n482 | \n483 |     Abs will always return a SymPy object.\n484 | \n485 |     Parameters\n486 |     ==========\n487 | \n488 |     arg : Expr\n489 |         Real or complex expression.\n490 | \n491 |     Returns\n492 |     =======\n493 | \n494 |     expr : Expr\n495 |         Absolute value returned can be an expression or integer depending on\n496 |         input arg.\n497 | \n498 |     See Also\n499 |     ========\n500 | \n501 |     sign, conjugate\n502 |     \"\"\"\n503 | \n504 |     args: tTuple[Expr]\n505 | \n506 |     is_extended_real = True\n507 |     is_extended_negative = False\n508 |     is_extended_nonnegative = True\n509 |     unbranched = True\n510 |     _singularities = True  # non-holomorphic\n511 | \n512 |     def fdiff(self, argindex=1):\n513 |         \"\"\"\n514 |         Get the first derivative of the argument to Abs().\n515 | \n516 |         \"\"\"\n517 |         if argindex == 1:\n518 |             return sign(self.args[0])\n519 |         else:\n520 |             raise ArgumentIndexError(self, argindex)\n521 | \n522 |     @classmethod\n523 |     def eval(cls, arg):\n524 |         from sympy.simplify.simplify import signsimp\n525 | \n526 |         if hasattr(arg, '_eval_Abs'):\n527 |             obj = arg._eval_Abs()\n528 |             if obj is not None:\n529 |                 return obj\n530 |         if not isinstance(arg, Expr):\n531 |             raise TypeError(\"Bad argument type for Abs(): %s\" % type(arg))\n532 | \n533 |         # handle what we can\n534 |         arg = signsimp(arg, evaluate=False)\n535 |         n, d = arg.as_numer_denom()\n536 |         if d.free_symbols and not n.free_symbols:\n537 |             return cls(n)/cls(d)\n538 | \n539 |         if arg.is_Mul:\n540 |             known = []\n541 |             unk = []\n542 |             for t in arg.args:\n543 |                 if t.is_Pow and t.exp.is_integer and t.exp.is_negative:\n544 |                     bnew = cls(t.base)\n545 |                     if isinstance(bnew, cls):\n546 |                         unk.append(t)\n547 |                     else:\n548 |                         known.append(Pow(bnew, t.exp))\n549 |                 else:\n550 |                     tnew = cls(t)\n551 |                     if isinstance(tnew, cls):\n552 |                         unk.append(t)\n553 |                     else:\n554 |                         known.append(tnew)\n555 |             known = Mul(*known)\n556 |             unk = cls(Mul(*unk), evaluate=False) if unk else S.One\n557 |             return known*unk\n558 |         if arg is S.NaN:\n559 |             return S.NaN\n560 |         if arg is S.ComplexInfinity:\n561 |             return oo\n562 |         from sympy.functions.elementary.exponential import exp, log\n563 | \n564 |         if arg.is_Pow:\n565 |             base, exponent = arg.as_base_exp()\n566 |             if base.is_extended_real:\n567 |                 if exponent.is_integer:\n568 |                     if exponent.is_even:\n569 |                         return arg\n570 |                     if base is S.NegativeOne:\n571 |                         return S.One\n572 |                     return Abs(base)**exponent\n573 |                 if base.is_extended_nonnegative:\n574 |                     return base**re(exponent)\n575 |                 if base.is_extended_negative:\n576 |                     return (-base)**re(exponent)*exp(-pi*im(exponent))\n577 |                 return\n578 |             elif not base.has(Symbol): # complex base\n579 |                 # express base**exponent as exp(exponent*log(base))\n580 |                 a, b = log(base).as_real_imag()\n581 |                 z = a + I*b\n582 |                 return exp(re(exponent*z))\n583 |         if isinstance(arg, exp):\n584 |             return exp(re(arg.args[0]))\n585 |         if isinstance(arg, AppliedUndef):\n586 |             if arg.is_positive:\n587 |                 return arg\n588 |             elif arg.is_negative:\n589 |                 return -arg\n590 |             return\n591 |         if arg.is_Add and arg.has(oo, S.NegativeInfinity):\n592 |             if any(a.is_infinite for a in arg.as_real_imag()):\n593 |                 return oo\n594 |         if arg.is_zero:\n595 |             return S.Zero\n596 |         if arg.is_extended_nonnegative:\n597 |             return arg\n598 |         if arg.is_extended_nonpositive:\n599 |             return -arg\n600 |         if arg.is_imaginary:\n601 |             arg2 = -I * arg\n602 |             if arg2.is_extended_nonnegative:\n603 |                 return arg2\n604 |         if arg.is_extended_real:\n605 |             return\n606 |         # reject result if all new conjugates are just wrappers around\n607 |         # an expression that was already in the arg\n608 |         conj = signsimp(arg.conjugate(), evaluate=False)\n609 |         new_conj = conj.atoms(conjugate) - arg.atoms(conjugate)\n610 |         if new_conj and all(arg.has(i.args[0]) for i in new_conj):\n611 |             return\n612 |         if arg != conj and arg != -conj:\n613 |             ignore = arg.atoms(Abs)\n614 |             abs_free_arg = arg.xreplace({i: Dummy(real=True) for i in ignore})\n615 |             unk = [a for a in abs_free_arg.free_symbols if a.is_extended_real is None]\n616 |             if not unk or not all(conj.has(conjugate(u)) for u in unk):\n617 |                 return sqrt(expand_mul(arg*conj))\n618 | \n619 |     def _eval_is_real(self):\n620 |         if self.args[0].is_finite:\n621 |             return True\n622 | \n623 |     def _eval_is_integer(self):\n624 |         if self.args[0].is_extended_real:\n625 |             return self.args[0].is_integer\n626 | \n627 |     def _eval_is_extended_nonzero(self):\n628 |         return fuzzy_not(self._args[0].is_zero)\n629 | \n630 |     def _eval_is_zero(self):\n631 |         return self._args[0].is_zero\n632 | \n633 |     def _eval_is_extended_positive(self):\n634 |         return fuzzy_not(self._args[0].is_zero)\n635 | \n636 |     def _eval_is_rational(self):\n637 |         if self.args[0].is_extended_real:\n638 |             return self.args[0].is_rational\n639 | \n640 |     def _eval_is_even(self):\n641 |         if self.args[0].is_extended_real:\n642 |             return self.args[0].is_even\n643 | \n644 |     def _eval_is_odd(self):\n645 |         if self.args[0].is_extended_real:\n646 |             return self.args[0].is_odd\n647 | \n648 |     def _eval_is_algebraic(self):\n649 |         return self.args[0].is_algebraic\n650 | \n651 |     def _eval_power(self, exponent):\n652 |         if self.args[0].is_extended_real and exponent.is_integer:\n653 |             if exponent.is_even:\n654 |                 return self.args[0]**exponent\n655 |             elif exponent is not S.NegativeOne and exponent.is_Integer:\n656 |                 return self.args[0]**(exponent - 1)*self\n657 |         return\n658 | \n659 |     def _eval_nseries(self, x, n, logx, cdir=0):\n660 |         from sympy.functions.elementary.exponential import log\n661 |         direction = self.args[0].leadterm(x)[0]\n662 |         if direction.has(log(x)):\n663 |             direction = direction.subs(log(x), logx)\n664 |         s = self.args[0]._eval_nseries(x, n=n, logx=logx)\n665 |         return (sign(direction)*s).expand()\n666 | \n667 |     def _eval_derivative(self, x):\n668 |         if self.args[0].is_extended_real or self.args[0].is_imaginary:\n669 |             return Derivative(self.args[0], x, evaluate=True) \\\n670 |                 * sign(conjugate(self.args[0]))\n671 |         rv = (re(self.args[0]) * Derivative(re(self.args[0]), x,\n672 |             evaluate=True) + im(self.args[0]) * Derivative(im(self.args[0]),\n673 |                 x, evaluate=True)) / Abs(self.args[0])\n674 |         return rv.rewrite(sign)\n675 | \n676 |     def _eval_rewrite_as_Heaviside(self, arg, **kwargs):\n677 |         # Note this only holds for real arg (since Heaviside is not defined\n678 |         # for complex arguments).\n679 |         from sympy.functions.special.delta_functions import Heaviside\n680 |         if arg.is_extended_real:\n681 |             return arg*(Heaviside(arg) - Heaviside(-arg))\n682 | \n683 |     def _eval_rewrite_as_Piecewise(self, arg, **kwargs):\n684 |         if arg.is_extended_real:\n685 |             return Piecewise((arg, arg >= 0), (-arg, True))\n686 |         elif arg.is_imaginary:\n687 |             return Piecewise((I*arg, I*arg >= 0), (-I*arg, True))\n688 | \n689 |     def _eval_rewrite_as_sign(self, arg, **kwargs):\n690 |         return arg/sign(arg)\n691 | \n692 |     def _eval_rewrite_as_conjugate(self, arg, **kwargs):\n693 |         return sqrt(arg*conjugate(arg))\n```\n"}, {"content": "```\nsympy/core/basic.py\nfunction: Basic.rewrite\n\nsympy/core/expr.py\nfunction: Expr.rewrite\n\nsympy/functions/elementary/complexes.py\nclass: Abs\nfunction: _eval_rewrite_as_Abs\n\nsympy/core/sign.py\nfunction: sign\n\nsympy/core/sympify.py\nfunction: sympify\n```\n<finish></finish>", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "finish", "description": "Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task."}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search_code_snippets", "description": "Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"search_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search."}, "line_nums": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}, "description": "Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search."}, "file_path_or_pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.", "default": "**/*.py"}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_entity_contents", "description": "\nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"entity_names": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py')."}}, "required": ["entity_names"]}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "explore_tree_structure", "description": "\nUnified repository exploring tool that traverses a pre-built code graph to retrieve dependency structure around specified entities.\nThe search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n\nCode Graph Definition:\n* Entity Types: 'directory', 'file', 'class', 'function'.\n* Dependency Types: 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits'.\n* Hierarchy:\n    - Directories contain files and subdirectories.\n    - Files contain classes and functions.\n    - Classes contain inner classes and methods.\n    - Functions can contain inner functions.\n* Interactions:\n    - Files/classes/functions can import classes and functions.\n    - Classes can inherit from other classes.\n    - Classes and functions can invoke others (invocations in a class's `__init__` are attributed to the class).\nEntity ID:\n* Unique identifier including file path and module path.\n* Here's an example of an Entity ID: `\"interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func\"` identifies function `inner_func` within `method_a` of class `C` in `\"interface/C.py\"`.\n\nNotes:\n* Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n* Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n\n\nExample Usage:\n1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n        direction='downstream',\n        traversal_depth=2,\n        dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n\n2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n        direction='upstream',\n        traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n3. Exploring Repository Structure:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n      start_entities=['/'],\n      traversal_depth=2,\n      dependency_type_filter=['contains']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the tree repository structure from the root directory (/), traversing up to two levels deep and focusing only on 'contains' relationship.\n4. Generate Class Diagrams:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=selected_entity_ids,\n        direction='both',\n        traverse_depth=-1,\n        dependency_type_filter=['inherits']\n    )\n    ```\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"start_entities": {"description": "List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\nEntities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\" (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\nFor files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).", "type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "direction": {"description": "Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are: `upstream`, `downstream`, `both`.\n- 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n- 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others (how others depend on them).\n- 'both': Traversal on both direction.", "type": "string", "enum": ["upstream", "downstream", "both"], "default": "downstream"}, "traversal_depth": {"description": "Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).", "type": "integer", "default": 2}, "entity_type_filter": {"description": "List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal. If None, all entity types are included.", "type": ["array", "null"], "items": {"type": "string"}, "default": null}, "dependency_type_filter": {"description": "List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal. If None, all dependency types are included.", "type": ["array", "null"], "items": {"type": "string"}, "default": null}}, "required": ["start_entities"]}}}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 66566, "completion_tokens": 540}, "time": 33.27432703971863}]}}
{"instance_id": "pandas-dev__pandas-60187", "found_files": [["pandas/core/arrays/datetimelike.py", "pandas/core/indexes/accessors.py", "pandas/core/generic.py", "pandas/core/series.py"]], "found_modules": [["pandas/core/frame.py:DataFrame", "pandas/core/frame.py:DataFrame.round", "pandas/core/arrays/arrow/array.py:ArrowExtensionArray", "pandas/io/formats/style_render.py:_parse_latex_css_conversion", "pandas/core/arrays/categorical.py:Categorical", "pandas/io/pytables.py:IndexCol", "pandas/io/sql.py:SQLiteTable._register_date_adapters", "pandas/io/pytables.py:DataCol", "pandas/core/window/rolling.py:Rolling", "pandas/core/indexing.py:_ScalarAccessIndexer", "pandas/io/stata.py:_pad_bytes", "pandas/core/arrays/datetimelike.py:TimelikeOps", "pandas/core/accessor.py:PandasDelegate._add_delegate_accessors._create_delegator_method", "pandas/core/indexes/accessors.py:Properties", "pandas/core/arrays/datetimelike.py:validate_periods", "pandas/core/indexing.py:_iLocIndexer", "pandas/io/sql.py:SQLiteTable", "pandas/core/indexes/base.py:Index", "pandas/core/series.py:Series"]], "found_entities": [["pandas/core/frame.py:DataFrame.round", "pandas/core/frame.py:DataFrame.round._dict_round", "pandas/core/frame.py:DataFrame.round._series_round", "pandas/core/arrays/arrow/array.py:ArrowExtensionArray._str_normalize", "pandas/core/arrays/arrow/array.py:ArrowExtensionArray._str_rfind", "pandas/io/formats/style_render.py:_parse_latex_css_conversion.font_weight", "pandas/core/arrays/categorical.py:Categorical._codes", "pandas/io/pytables.py:IndexCol.kind_attr", "pandas/io/pytables.py:IndexCol.set_pos", "pandas/io/sql.py:SQLiteTable._register_date_adapters._adapt_time", "pandas/io/pytables.py:DataCol.dtype_attr", "pandas/core/window/rolling.py:Rolling.sem", "pandas/io/pytables.py:DataCol.meta_attr", "pandas/core/indexing.py:_ScalarAccessIndexer._convert_key", "pandas/core/window/rolling.py:Rolling.max", "pandas/io/pytables.py:IndexCol.itemsize", "pandas/core/arrays/categorical.py:Categori